cases={
'F001':dict(v='REFUTED',p='',epoch='',depth='',raw='Many narrow QLP dips recur at BTJD 1714.419, 1753.010, 1907.381, 1945.973, 1965.252 and again through S76; neighboring deep-event intervals give a conditional ~19.295 day train, distinctly different from Gaia 20.8768 days. The target-centered 3x3 S22 extraction still contains a diluted 6.47-ppt loss, so that extraction alone is not a source assignment.',physics='S22 difference centroid (6.241,5.744) is 0.12 pixel from the capable Gaia 1615623244847893376 (G flux ratio 0.534) and 1.33 pixels from the queued target. The repeated narrow shape and spatial displacement together favor the neighbor as the eclipsing source. Gaia conjunction at this event is not a persuasive target-orbit association; source-specific linear covariance is broad.',limits='Refutes attribution of the selected eclipse train to this queued target, not the target\'s possible independent binarity. Difference-centroid systematics and blended apertures remain.',next='Review the neighboring Gaia source as a separate candidate if useful.'),
'F002':dict(v='PROBABLE',p='59.919',epoch='1434.925',depth='5',raw='Resolved short raw SAP dips in S4 1434.925, S31 2153.951, S97 3951.547 and S98 4011.467 repeat at ~59.919 days. Target-centered TESSCut 3x3 extraction independently recovers ~4.31 ppt in S4. The S97 detector fit at 3941.719 is a separate spurious/broad feature and is excluded from the clock.',physics='Gaia AstroSpectroSB1 ABFG gives inclination 87.18 degrees and omega 158.09 degrees, near C/H check 155.11 degrees. Source-specific full-covariance A conjunction predicts 1434.712 +/- 0.224 d; observed offset +0.213 d. The nearest Gaia neighbor lies just 0.475 pixel away, G ratio 0.00315 versus measured 0.00431 fractional loss; nominal G-band total disappearance falls short, but TESS bandpass and aperture contributions prevent decisive exclusion.',limits='Real repeated eclipses and compatible Gaia geometry; close capable or near-capable neighbor prevents firm source ownership. Period is approximate from representative raw centers, not a calibrated ephemeris error.',next='Obtain higher-resolution eclipse photometry or a multi-pixel PSF fit across separate events.'),
'F003':dict(v='PLAUSIBLE',p='',epoch='2919.312',depth='190',raw='S59 contains one striking, resolved raw SAP eclipse-shaped loss near 2919.31, reaching about 20% at its center; an independent target-centered aperture shows a resolved ~4% diluted loss. S19, S73 and S86 show strong quasi-periodic oscillation and detector dip flags but no matching second deep eclipse at the inspected times.',physics='Gaia SB2 P56.391 days predicts a conjunction far from this event (nearest A offset ~0.1695 phase, 11.1 linear sigma), so the selected deep feature is not established as the Gaia orbit. A brighter Gaia neighbor at 1.05 pixels, G flux ratio 4.05, can produce the blended loss; the difference centroid near the target cannot resolve such a close blend.',limits='Only one secure deep event, no measured orbital clock, and close source attribution unresolved. Oscillatory minima are not counted as repeat eclipses.',next='Obtain another deep event with a resolved WCS/PSF source measurement.'),
'F004':dict(v='REFUTED',p='',epoch='',depth='',raw='The prominent S103 QLP window at 4164.905 is a long decline that runs into a data gap; S104 and earlier S95 flags likewise combine drifts with orbit-boundary steps or incomplete baselines. The valid target-centered S103 cutout gives -0.77 ppt in the chosen broad window, rather than the flagged 11-ppt deficit.',physics='The apparent selected dip does not have a complete eclipse core and egress in that observing occasion. A detector gap/step plus trending baseline positively explains why the broad screening statistic flagged it. Gaia SB2 conjunction offset at the provisional feature is large (~0.147 phase to nearest kind, ~7.2 linear sigma), but this is not the basis of refutation.',limits='Refutes these selected QLP windows as demonstrated target eclipses; does not prove absence of binarity or rule out a future resolved event.',next='Use a fully covered event in independent coverage before reconsidering eclipse status.'),
'F005':dict(v='REFUTED',p='',epoch='',depth='',raw='The highest S101 4112.997 window shows a decline into a gap with no resolved symmetric eclipse, while S103 and S94 flags involve similar boundary/trend structure. A valid target-centered S101 aperture has only ~0.98 ppt broad-window loss versus the QLP screened ~12 ppt; no repeated same-kind eclipse centers can be measured from these windows.',physics='The data gaps, sector steps and sloping baselines supply a positive non-eclipse explanation for the selected detector flags. Gaia P58.322 days does not rescue an unmeasured eclipse clock.',limits='Refutes the selected screening features, not all possible binary behavior; some lower-amplitude variation remains.',next='Review a future fully covered short dip if one appears.'),
'F006':dict(v='PROBABLE',p='39.44',epoch='3057.805',depth='25',raw='S64, S100 and S101 show independent broad raw SAP troughs near 3057.8, 4083.5 and 4122.9. The three-event overlay shows a steep ingress, approximately two-day flat core and steep egress on each occasion, with continuous ±0.5-day cores and two-sided sidebands; local diagnostic depths are ~24.7, 21.3 and 19.0 ppt. S100 has a short right sideband, so that depth is less robust. The S101 target-centered 3x3 extraction retains ~25 ppt deficit. Earlier S37 flag is rising variability, not a repeat timing point.',physics='The conditional S64-S100-S101 spacing is about 39.44 days, close to Gaia 39.436 days. Source-specific B conjunction at S64 is near the observed feature (+0.0092 phase, 0.63 linear sigma). Several neighbors are capable in the crowded aperture, so attribution is unresolved. ASAS-SN calls this target ROT, which does not negate the repeated flat-core eclipse morphology. An additional S65 TESSCut ends before the predicted event and has near-zero target counts, so it supplies no null.',limits='Crowding, sloping baselines and uncertain broad-event centers limit source ownership and period precision. No formal error is inferred from provisional trapezoid fits.',next='Obtain a clean fully covered next trough with spatial difference imaging and a second extraction.'),
'F007':dict(v='CONFIRMED',p='38.193',epoch='3132.851',depth='7',raw='Narrow, similar raw SAP eclipses occur in S1 1337.722, S67 3132.851, S68 3171.042, S94 3858.534 and S95 3896.731. The short 38.191-day consecutive spacing recurs after 18 cycles and again one cycle later, supporting ~38.193 days from measured centers; broad S103/S104 gap-edge features are excluded. The explicit shorter-period test finds four fully covered sensitive P/2 nulls, seven P/3 nulls and fourteen P/4 nulls in real q0 cadence windows. Representative visually inspected S94 P/2 BTJD3877.615 has 112 continuous core points, max gap 0.0023 d, -0.05 ppt local depth and 0.49-ppt sideband MAD noise; S67 P/3 BTJD3145.582 has 112 points, gap 0.0023 d, +0.06 ppt depth and 0.86-ppt noise, versus ~7-ppt real events. S1 target-centered TESSCut recovers a ~7.26-ppt resolved dip.',physics='S1 difference deficit is centered within ~0.20 pixel of target. The nearest nominally capable Gaia neighbor is 4.46 pixels away, so source location and aperture throughput strongly favor the target. Gaia SB1 P38.229 +/-0.040 days agrees with the measured clock within its period uncertainty; the S94 source-specific B/A conjunction comparison has a broad compatible A offset (-0.073 phase, -0.73 linear sigma).',limits='S1 timing covariance is singular; reported period is rounded from multiple good later events with no formal uncertainty. Shorter-alias nulls assume similar same-kind ~7-ppt eclipses. Alternative extraction shares TESS photons.',next='A later resolved eclipse would tighten the ephemeris.'),
'F008':dict(v='PROBABLE',p='50.0',epoch='3846.274',depth='414',raw='S93 has a deep ~41% target-centered eclipse at 3846.274; S12 contains a partial deep core and resolved egress near 1646, though its center is gapped. S39 2371.095 is a shallower full trough; S66 near 3121 has clear shallower ingress and partial bottom. These are four distinct occasions in two eclipse kinds, not four precise centers of one kind.',physics='Deep-kind separation is conditionally 44 cycles at ~50.0 days; shallow-kind separation is conditionally 15 cycles at ~50.0 days. Neighboring 43/45 and 14/16 cycle choices do not intersect within ~1-day partial-center bounds; deep k45 also predicts a fully covered S39 window near 2379.291 with only ~10 ppt rather than ~400 ppt. P/2 would map the deep S12 event onto the shallow S39 occasion under stable eclipse-kind morphology. Gaia SB1 P49.976 days and source-specific B conjunction near S93 (+0.042 phase, 0.53 linear sigma) are compatible. No Gaia neighbor in the cutout has enough G flux to explain half of the measured deficit. VSX V1291 Sco is an exact ~0.05-arcsec prior EA identification with P50.007 days; this is a known recovery, not a new EB.',limits='Partial events and possible morphology changes limit unique photometric cycle proof and prevent a precision period error; the VSX clock is corroboration, not the source of a claimed measured eclipse period.',next='Get another complete deep and shallow occasion to refine the photometric clock.'),
'F009':dict(v='REFUTED',p='',epoch='',depth='',raw='QLP S94 begins with an apparent ~30% low plateau and repeats a sharp rise near 3869 after an orbit gap. Raw S93/S103/S104 also show strong trends and boundary steps. A valid finite positive target-centered 3x3 S94 extraction stays roughly 0.95-1.02 normalized across both alleged giant events and has -0.52 ppt at the 3869 broad window.',physics='The giant QLP excursions are not present in the target-centered photons at commensurate depth; gap and baseline structure provide a positive extraction/contamination explanation. A brighter source at ~2.82 pixels is capable of contaminating QLP.',limits='Refutes selected giant eclipse attribution to queued target, not all low-level variability. The neighbor is not promoted without its own review.',next='If useful, review the off-target/contaminating source as a separate candidate.'),
'F010':dict(v='PROBABLE',p='',epoch='3833.891',depth='24',raw='Broad flat-bottom raw losses repeat in S12 ~1649.35, S39 ~2377.50, S66 ~3105.50 and S93 ~3833.89. Independent local sideband overlays show similar ~21-24-ppt core losses in each sector; S39 lacks most pre-baseline and S66 lacks post-baseline, so those depth figures are diagnostic only. A second, shallower kind appears around S12 1632.86 and S93 3850.35. S93 target-centered extraction retains a ~25-ppt broad trough.',physics='If the four deep events span 66 cycles, the conditional period is ~33.099 days; 63 and 69 cycles over the same baseline (~34.675 and ~31.660 days) also align the three ~728-day gaps, while bounded cache coverage has no strong null to reject them. The other-kind pair is too sparse to determine its cycle count independently. Gaia SB1 P33.089 days favors the 66-cycle alias and near-circular geometry predicts a B conjunction ~0.027 phase from S93 (1.8 linear sigma), but Gaia agreement is not a measured unique eclipse clock. Difference flux is within ~0.30 pixel of target; nominally capable Gaia neighbors are 3.7-4.4 pixels away and aperture throughput favors the target.',limits='Real repeated broad eclipses have unresolved integer aliases; no measured orbital period is adopted. S39/S66 incomplete baselines and same-photon alternative extraction limit depth and source precision.',next='Observe a deep eclipse at a discriminating intermediate cycle and repeat target-centered pixels.')
}
fields=['sample_id','tic','sample_stratum','physical_verdict','full_review_completion','novelty_status','period_days','period_error_days','primary_epoch_btjd','depth_ppt','limitations','next_check','report']
status={r['sample_id']:r for r in csv.DictReader((B.parent/'shared-catalogues'/'TARGET-STATUS.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf8'))}
